      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In such scenarios, risks often emerge from tensions over resource allocation, particularly in local schools or municipal services, leading to perceived competition. Differences in social norms, language barriers, and differing expectations regarding public behavior can create friction, challenging the established \"neighborhood character.\" Furthermore, if existing residents feel their cultural dominance is being challenged without inclusive dialogue, feelings of alienation and resentment can erode existing social harmony. Addressing these requires intentional community-building efforts that validate both incoming and long-term residents.",
      "source": "target",
